# Scalability & High Availability

**Scalability** means a system can handle more workload as demand increases.
- **Vertical scaling (scale up/down)** — make one server more powerful by adding more CPU, RAM, or storage.
- **Horizontal scaling (scale out/in) = Elasticity** — add or remove more servers/instances to handle demand.

**High Availability (HA)** means keeping the application running even if part of the system fails.

### Simple Difference
```text
Scalability
→ Can the system handle more load?

High Availability
→ Can the system stay online when something fails?
```

Example:
```text
Vertical scaling → Upgrade one worker's tools
Horizontal scaling → Hire more workers
High Availability → Have backups so work continues if someone is unavailable
```

A system can be highly available without scaling well, and vice versa.